% ============================================================================
% moon-phase-ordinal-position — each Moon phase's place in the lunar cycle as
% an ORDINAL WORD ("fifth", not just "5"), DERIVED (not looked up) by
% bridging an astronomy fact to a math fact (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY,
% cross-directory composition with MATHEMATICS). Grounds the common framing
% "the full Moon is the FIFTH phase in the cycle."
% ============================================================================
% `astronomy/moon-phases.adj` already tables each Moon phase's position in the
% lunar cycle as a NUMBER ("full_moon" is position 5); `mathematics/ordinal-
% numbers.adj` already tables each NUMBER's ordinal WORD (5 is "fifth").
% Neither file states outright "the full Moon is the fifth phase" — but
% composing the two ALREADY-SHIPPED facts answers it directly. This is the
% SAME "compose two independently-citable facts into a derived, dual-cited
% conclusion" discipline `language/word-families.adj`,
% `earth-science/season-start-month-number.adj`, and
% `astronomy/planet-ordinal-position.adj` already established — the THIRD
% cross-directory `rule` composition in this library, and the SECOND time
% this exact number-to-ordinal-word bridge pattern has been applied (the
% first was `planet-ordinal-position.adj`, to a DIFFERENT already-shipped
% table in the SAME `astronomy/` directory), demonstrating the pattern
% generalizes to any already-shipped `(thing, number)` table with zero new
% engine work.
%
%     import "moon-phase-ordinal-position.adj"
%     ? moon_phase_ordinal_position(full_moon, $O)      % fifth
%     ? moon_phase_ordinal_position($P, eighth)         % waning_crescent (reverse recall)
%     ? moon_phase_ordinal_position(eclipse, $O)        % abstain -- not one of the 8 tabled phases
%
% This is a native `rule` (ADJ-TABLES RS-6): its body binds `$Phase`/`$Number`
% through the already-shipped `moon_phase_order` table, then re-binds
% `$Number`/`$Ordinal` through the already-shipped `ordinal_number` table -- a
% plain two-hop join (the same shape `planet-ordinal-position.adj` already
% established), not a causal inference; it just names the standard convention
% that a counting number has a corresponding ordinal word. A query's `steps`
% trail shows THREE entries: the rule's own citation (the ordinal-word
% convention), the `moon_phase_order` fact's citation (NASA), and the
% `ordinal_number` fact's citation, so a human can independently verify each
% link. Honest abstention on any word `moon-phases.adj` does not table -- an
% eclipse is a different astronomical event, deliberately NOT a row in the
% source table, so this rule abstains on it too, rather than inventing a
% position.
%
% CROSS-DIRECTORY IMPORT: this file lives in `astronomy/` (its natural
% subject home, alongside `moon-phases.adj`) and imports its mathematics
% sibling with a relative `../mathematics/ordinal-numbers.adj` path -- the
% SAME shape `planet-ordinal-position.adj` and, before it,
% `earth-science/season-start-month-number.adj` already established and
% empirically verified: the CLI's import sandbox is rooted at the TOP-LEVEL
% PROGRAM's own directory, not each importer's directory, so this resolves
% cleanly provided the consuming entry program lives at or above the package
% root. This library's OWN `.query.adj` companion is therefore placed at the
% package root (not beside this file), mirroring
% `mathematics/word-problems.query.adj`, `season-start-month-number.query.adj`,
% and `planet-ordinal-position.query.adj`'s already-established pattern.
%
% Deliberately scoped to the STANDARD ordinal-word bridge only, over the
% canonical eight lunar phases `moon-phases.adj` already tables -- no new
% phase or ordinal-number content is asserted here, only the join. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

import "moon-phases.adj"
import "../mathematics/ordinal-numbers.adj"

rule { head: moon_phase_ordinal_position($Phase, $Ordinal)
       when: moon_phase_order($Phase, $Number), ordinal_number($Number, $Ordinal)
       source "1 one first, 2 two second, 3 three third, 4 four fourth, 5 five fifth"
       locator "https://www.ef.edu/english-resources/english-grammar/numbers-english/"
       trust consensus }
